  "final_text": "None of the eleven files works as evidence for R7. Tannwald asks for \"a restore test of your backups from the last 12 months, with its result\", and our folder has no record of one. The backup report says outright that none was done.\n\n**What's in the folder:**\n\n- **`backup-report_2026-10.txt`** is the only file that deals with backups. It shows that 31 of 31 nightly jobs completed in October, and its owner is named (Goran Petrovski). But it also says: *\"Restore test: not done in 2026. Planned for Q4 2026 (owner: IT lead).\"* Jobs completing is not the same as a restore test, and Tannwald's rule is \"send the record that shows it was done.\"\n- **`it-notes_2026-10-06.md`** says \"Backups are tested, the jobs run every night.\" This is a personal note that nobody reviewed, and it describes what we do rather than recording it. The backup report also contradicts it. Don't send it.\n- **`infosec-policy_v3_FINAL.md`** says \"Backups are restore-tested twice a year.\" That's a rule, not a record. It was approved in March 2024, its review was due in March 2025, and it falls before the 10 December 2025 cutoff. It also makes the gap look worse, because our own policy requires tests we haven't run.\n- **`subprocessors_2026-09.csv`** only lists Nordlager as the backup provider.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n\n1. **Run the restore test now and record it.** The deadline is 25 November, so there's time. A restore of the Servicepult export and the file share from Nordlager would do. The record should give the date, what was restored, the result (for example, files complete and the data matches), and who did it and signed it off. Send that record as R7.\n2. **If the test can't happen in time, say so honestly.** Tell Tannwald no restore test was done in the last 12 months and give the planned date. Don't send the October report as if it showed a test. It says the opposite, and Tannwald will read it.\n\nSeparately: the IT notes also point to problems that matter for